  "body_text": "96 \r\n👑 Plus: Medieval majesty | Monday, August 10, 2026\r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n Axios Finish Line By\r\nMike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei\r\n &middot; Aug 10, 2026 \r\nAug 10, 2026\r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Tonight's host is Axios publisher Nicholas Johnston, sharing how AI can help bookworms remember the plot, skip the spoilers and tackle dense prose.\n\n Smart Brevity™ count: 428 words … 1½ mins. Edited by Natalie Daher and copy edited by Amy Stern. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n1 big thing: Don&#x27;t lose the plot \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n AI has become my reading buddy as I've plowed through my summer book list, helping me keep track of plots and characters, unpack themes, and make sense of wordy, old prose, Axios' Nicholas Johnston writes. \n\n Why it matters: The way many of us actually read books — a few pages before bed, a chapter at the beach, another on a plane — makes it easy to lose the thread. AI can help you pick it back up without starting over. 📖 I really got into using AI while reading James Fenimore Cooper's \"The Last of the Mohicans.\" The text was far too dense for casual vacation reading. My first prompt asked:\n\n \"I'd like a guide as I read The Last of the Mohicans who can help me understand what I've read and where the story is, without giving spoilers or getting ahead of my progress. Can you do this, chapter by chapter?\" The robot agreed: \"Cooper's sentences are long and his diction is archaic.\" It promised to give me \"a recap of what's happened so far and clarify anything confusing. ... I'll stick strictly to what you've read. No hints about what's coming.\" 🔖 That's now become part of my reading routine: Tell AI where you stopped and ask for a spoiler-free recap so far, which often surfaces details I'd missed or forgotten.\n\n 🤔 Reality check: AI is working from its training data, not the actual text. The vibes can be right while the details are wrong.\n\n After I read James Jones' \"The Thin Red Line,\" AI confused Captain Stein with Captain Staros, the corresponding character in the movie adaptation. Pro tip: If the book is old enough to be in the public domain, tell AI to find the online text so it can be more accurate with its answers. \"The Last of the Mohicans,\" for example, is available through Project Gutenberg. \n\n\r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA message from Axios\r\n\n\r\n \r\n Don't Miss: Our Quarterly Report on Ad Economics \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive just released its exclusive quarterly report on the forces reshaping ad economics.\n\n\r\n \r\n Built for executives, it pairs expert reporting with essential data and charts to show where ad dollars are moving next. \r\n \r\n 🔒 Become a member today to unlock this exclusive Media Trends Executive report.\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n2. 🏰 Parting shot: Castle cruise\r\n \r\n \r\n Photo: Steve Ward Steve Ward of Plymouth, Mich., shares this view of the Gravensteen while vacationing in Ghent, Belgium, earlier this month.\n\n The medieval castle dates to 1180, and once served as the residence of the Counts of Flanders. 🍺 Ward also points to a favorite bit of local lore: In 1949, students occupied the empty castle in protest of a new beer tax, staying overnight before the standoff ended peacefully the next morning.\n\n\r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n\r\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
